content='''<div class="page-intro"><div class="eyebrow">20 October 2026 UTC · CAM v0.1 / CD-1 v0.1</div><h1>A canonical map, then a measurable diamond.</h1><p class="lede">The ideal apparatus map fixes the action scale, energy scale and clock. A four-link component test checks reciprocal matter dynamics and local Gauss balance.</p></div>
<aside class="callout"><strong>Conditional theorem · Proposed apparatus test · Physical promotion 0</strong><p>Sixty-four exact constructor checks pass. The eight-page manuscript gives the proof and proposed test conditions. Actual apparatus calibration, non-constructor review and experimental execution remain open.</p></aside>
<div class="actions"><a class="button secondary" href="PDF_PATH">Read theorem and test PDF ↗</a><a class="text-link" href="ZIP_PATH">Source and protocol package ↓</a><a class="text-link" href="JSON_PATH">Machine-readable CD-1 protocol</a></div>
<section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">01</div><div><h2>The apparatus-to-model map.</h2><p>For each physical flux–charge pair, choose scales Φ₀ and Q₀ with a common product Φ₀Q₀=S₀. If the retained apparatus Hamiltonian is E₀ times the target Hamiltonian, the model clock is τ=tE₀/S₀. Invertible readout gains alone do not establish this canonical structure or dynamics.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Physical record</th><th>Model coordinate</th><th>Required property</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Link flux Φ, signed charge Q</td><td>θ=Φ/Φ₀; E=Q/Q₀; U=exp(iθ)</td><td>Independent angle and signed momentum; periodic coupling</td></tr>
<tr><td>First matter flux–charge pair</td><td>ψ=(Φ/Φ₀+iQ/Q₀)/√2</td><td>Complex bracket −i in model units</td></tr>
<tr><td>Second matter flux–charge pair</td><td>w=(Φ/Φ₀−iQ/Q₀)/√2</td><td>Complex bracket +i; w is the conjugated canonical second species</td></tr>
<tr><td>Calibrated action S₀ and energy E₀</td><td>t₀=S₀/E₀ in seconds</td><td>All channel action products agree within a bounded mismatch</td></tr>
</tbody></table><p>The two species can use ordinary canonical hardware pairs. The reversed complex convention supplies the second species sign. The normalized model electric variable is not automatically a laboratory Maxwell field, and its Gauss generator is not yet an identified electric-charge law.</p></div></section>
<section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">02</div><div><h2>One instrumented incidence diamond.</h2><p>Nodes are 0, 01, 02 and 012. Links follow the two downward paths from face 012 to vertex 0. Their incidence signs are b=(−1,1,−1,−1); the cycle is c=(1,1,−1,−1). The graph has four nodes, four links and one loop.</p><p>Record 24 state coordinates plus eight outputs measuring the complex couplings independently. A computed exp(iθ) always has unit modulus; it cannot validate the actual coupling. Record clocks, applied sources, feedback, probe disturbance and joint calibration uncertainty.</p><p>For isolated gauge-field calibration, the loop phase satisfies φ″=−4aκ sin φ. Its finite-amplitude period follows an elliptic integral, so a finite-amplitude measurement must use that prediction rather than its linear limit.</p></div></section>
<section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">03</div><div><h2>Seven condition families with discriminating controls.</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Measured signature</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>A · Signed rotor response</td><td>Constant electric cycle kick; loop phase slope 4ε</td></tr>
<tr><td>B · Plaquette response</td><td>Finite-amplitude pendulum; compare with plaquette coupling disabled</td></tr>
<tr><td>C · Reciprocal matter dynamics</td><td>Electric acceleration −b, both continuity laws and conserved Gauss values</td></tr>
<tr><td>D · Relative equilibrium and kick</td><td>Opposite matter phase rotation, loop holonomy −1, then predicted cycle-kick response</td></tr>
<tr><td>E · Known source</td><td>One-species radial loss produces the specified local source and trace return</td></tr>
<tr><td>F · Changed local frame</td><td>Equal invariant histories, correctly transformed raw trajectories</td></tr>
<tr><td>G · Blinded faults</td><td>Wrong species sign gives initial Gauss slope (2,−2,0,0); disabled electric response gives (−2,2,0,0)</td></tr>
</tbody></table><p>The proposed component coefficients are a=1, κ=1/16, μ=0 and g=1, with g=0 for calibration. They are declared component values. The diamond is not an invariant subsystem carrying the full tetrahedral trapping theorem.</p></div></section>
<section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">04</div><div><h2>A concrete proposed measurement rule.</h2><p>CD-1 proposes 20 independent preparations per condition, randomized and blinded, with model sample interval at most 1/1000 and horizon 8π. Physical time and sample rate are chosen after t₀ calibration. These counts are an initial design, not a power calculation.</p><p>Engineering targets are 1/100 in the declared dimensionless state, source, Gauss, coupling and energy decision channels, with channel action mismatch at most 1/1000. Use the exact bounds and prefix rules in the protocol; these targets do not replace the earlier endpoint theorem tolerances.</p><p>Supply at least 99% joint coverage for every quantity used in a run’s decision. Missing calibration, unresolved controls, saturation, missing channels or unbounded reconstruction/solver error yields HOLD. A sampled residual does not establish a continuous-time bound without an intersample certificate. Weak trajectory agreement alone does not establish the SDW absolute defect path or energy-work bounds.</p><p>The next physical return is an actual Hamiltonian/actuator design, calibrated action and energy scales, attainable error allocation, validated reconstruction and attributable non-constructor review. No experiment has been executed.</p></div></section>
<section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">05</div><div><h2>Dataset watch.</h2><p>A weekly watch checks for improved experimental gauge-simulator releases on Monday mornings in Chicago time. It prioritizes raw phase/electric/matter records, closed loops, source tests and calibration. It sends updates only for meaningful new releases. The watch does not publish changes or contact researchers.</p><a class="text-link" href="/evidence/measurable-audit/">Read the published-dataset audit →</a></div></section>'''
